<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="Hike planner" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1">
  <metadata>
    <name>Mount Si</name>
    <desc>Mount Si trailhead to the Haystack basin and back. 12.2 km, +989 m, −987 m.</desc>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="47.488172" lon="-121.723142">
    <ele>202.8</ele>
    <name>1 Trailhead</name>
    <desc>Discover Pass on the dashboard. Water and toilets at the trailhead; none on the trail, so carry 1.5–2 L each. The lot holds about 200 cars and fills on sunny weekends.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.496585" lon="-121.733870">
    <ele>629.7</ele>
    <name>2 Snag Flats</name>
    <desc>The one flat stretch: a boardwalk through big old trees and fire-scarred snags. Almost half the distance, but only a third of the climb.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.499059" lon="-121.738314">
    <ele>899.7</ele>
    <name>3 Upper switchbacks, 900 m</name>
    <desc>Steady 15–18% grade, switchback after switchback. Eat now; the last 300 m of climb are the steepest.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.504093" lon="-121.740656">
    <ele>1132.0</ele>
    <name>4 Old Si Trail junction</name>
    <desc>The Old Si Trail comes in from the left. Trees thin out and the trail turns rocky. Layer up before the top: it is usually colder and windier than the forest.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.506247" lon="-121.738634">
    <ele>1192.7</ele>
    <name>5 Top of the trail, Haystack basin</name>
    <desc>The summit for most hikers: views over North Bend, Mailbox Peak to the east, Rainier to the south. The Haystack above is an exposed scramble (+85 m, steep rock, real drop-offs). Skip it when wet or icy, or without scrambling experience.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.504093" lon="-121.740656">
    <ele>1132.0</ele>
    <name>6 Old Si Trail junction (down)</name>
    <desc>Stay on the main Mount Si Trail. The Old Si Trail is steeper and rougher; easy to take by mistake on the way down.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.496585" lon="-121.733870">
    <ele>629.7</ele>
    <name>7 Snag Flats (down)</name>
    <desc>Flat ground for a few minutes; the rest of the descent is steady. Shorten poles and keep the steps small.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="47.488172" lon="-121.723142">
    <ele>202.8</ele>
    <name>8 Trailhead</name>
    <desc>Finish. Back at the car.</desc>
    <sym>Flag, Blue</sym>
    <type>Milestone</type>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>Mount Si</name>
